# Docker build context — deny by default.
#
# This file excludes everything and then re-admits only what `go build` needs.
# The allowlist direction is deliberate: an ignore list has to predict every
# secret-bearing path that might appear, and it silently fails the day one
# appears that nobody predicted. This inverts that — a new file is outside the
# build context until someone adds it here on purpose.
#
# What the deny-by-default rule actually keeps out of the context today:
#
#   .git/                                 19 MB of history, every reverted
#                                         experiment and every commit message
#   test/integration/*/env/certs/         generated CA and server PRIVATE KEYS
#                                         for the PostgreSQL, Kafka and Redpanda
#                                         fixtures — untracked, but present in a
#                                         developer tree that has run `make
#                                         integration-*`, which is exactly the
#                                         tree an image gets built from
#   .claude/, CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md        local agent instructions
#   .env, *.pem, *.key, credentials*      whatever a working tree accumulated
#   .DS_Store                             macOS noise
#
# The build needs four things and no more: the module files and the two Go
# source trees. There are no `go:embed` assets, no generated code and no
# codegen step, so nothing else belongs in the context.
#
# `docs/`, `test/` and `scripts/` are intentionally absent from the allowlist.
# The image ships a binary, not the repository.
*

!go.mod
!go.sum
!cmd/
!internal/

# Re-exclude anything that could ride in under the two admitted source trees.
# `!cmd/` and `!internal/` re-admit those directories wholesale, so these rules
# exist to keep a stray secret or a macOS artefact from being carried along.
**/.DS_Store
**/testdata/**/*.pem
**/testdata/**/*.key
